# Chapter One: The Night the Light Went Out

## The keeper's kitchen
@scene ambience="rain:-2, wind:-8, fire:-12" space=kitchen
@music start home prompt="gentle acoustic guitar and soft cello, cosy, warm, rainy evening, slow" gain=-2 fade=5

On a crooked little island called {Gullhaven|GULL-hay-ven}, at the very edge of the map, there stood a lighthouse with a red door and a kettle that was never quite cold.

Mira had come to stay for the whole summer. On the third night, the sky decided to put on a show.
@sfx thunder distance=0.7 gain=-4
@pause 1.2

MIRA (bored 0.7): Grandpa Tom, there is nothing to do. The rain has been raining for a hundred years. <sigh>
TOM (dry_humor 0.6): Three hours, love. It only feels like a hundred when you count the drops.
MIRA (peeved 0.5): I wasn't counting. [cheeky 0.5] I was up to four thousand and twelve.

Grandpa Tom chuckled into his tea, and the fire answered him with a pop.

TOM (warm 0.6): Tell you what. When the storm settles, I'll show you how the great lamp turns. Not many people get to see that.
MIRA (excited 0.8): Really? Right up at the top? Promise?
TOM (dry_humor 0.5): I never promise anything before my second biscuit.
@pause 0.6

@music stop fade=3
@sfx thunder distance=0.15 gain=2 wait=true
@ambience remove fire fade=0.6

And then, all at once, the kitchen went dark. The lamp at the top of the tower, the one that had shone every single night for sixty years, went out.

MIRA (whisper, afraid 0.7): Grandpa? Grandpa, the light. The ships can't see us.
TOM (calm 0.7, authoritative 0.4): Steady now. Take my hand. We'll go up together, one step at a time.

## The stairs
@scene ambience="wind_storm:-6, rain_heavy:-10" space=stairwell
@music start climb prompt="low tense strings, slow heartbeat pulse, suspense, dark" gain=-4 fade=6

There were one hundred and twelve steps to the lantern room. Mira counted every one of them, out loud, in a very small voice.

MIRA (whisper, anxious 0.6): Ninety-nine. One hundred. [whisper, afraid 0.8] Grandpa, something is moving up there.
TOM (soft, mysterious 0.5): Probably just the wind. [dry_humor 0.4] Or a ghost with very poor manners.
@sfx knock count=3 gain=-6 wait=true
MIRA (scream 0.8): Aaah!

## The lantern room
@scene ambience="wind:-6, rain:-10" space=small_room
@music stop fade=2

PIP (peeved 0.8, projected): Do you mind? Some of us are trying to sleep! This is a private lantern. Seagulls only.
MIRA (surprised 0.8): A seagull? [laughing] A talking seagull!
PIP (smug 0.6): Not just any seagull. Pip. Keeper of the Lantern, Second Class. Very important position.
TOM (dry_humor 0.7): He's been squatting up here since Easter. He pays his rent in feathers.

The great lamp sat silent in its brass cradle. The wick had burned clean through.

TOM (tired 0.6): My fingers aren't what they were. You'll have to thread it, Mira. Through the little ring, and tie it off.
MIRA (frustrated 0.7): It won't go! It keeps slipping. <groan> Why won't this silly knot just behave?
PIP (cheeky 0.7): Over, under, around the moon. Even a seagull knows that.
MIRA (determined 0.7): Over... under... around the moon. [triumphant 0.8] I did it!

Grandpa Tom struck a match. The flame caught, the great lens began to turn, and a long golden beam swept out across the angry sea.
@sfx sparkle gain=-6
@music start beam prompt="soaring warm orchestra, french horn and strings, hopeful, triumphant, sunrise" gain=0 fade=2

Far out on the water, a ship's bell rang twice.
@sfx bell pitch=330 gain=-8
@sfx bell pitch=330 gain=-8 offset=1.1

MIRA (awe 0.8): Grandpa... they saw us. They can see the way home.
TOM (proud 0.6, tender 0.4): That they can, love. That they can.
